It is governed by five factors articulated by the Apex Court in Laxman Naskar v. Union of India, (2000) 2 SCC 595, which are:
(i) Whether the offense is an individual act of crime without affecting society at large;
(ii) Whether there is any chance of future recurrence and whether the convict has lost potentiality for committing crime;
(iii) Whether there is any fruitful purpose of confining this convict anymore;
(iv) Whether there is any fruitful purpose of confining this convict anymore; and (v) Socio-economic condition of the convict's family.
